<Defs>

  <!--
    The Quiet Bell (docs/design/peoples-next.md, the second of the eight; docs/design/peoples.md, "Release D";
    Source/ColonyCouncil/Peoples/PeoplesD.cs): keepers of the dead of every people, friend or foe, who listen to the ground
    for what should not stir. Medieval, on the game's tribal parent; they arise in every age (ariseAges 0~3), and a
    people of their ways in the industrial or spacer age carries flamers for what will not stay down (ageArms). Their
    bell-keepers share an undyed grey habit and veil (the habit's rule: one habit, its cut free); their sextons and
    gravewardens dress plainly and differently around a grey hood or cape. Their towns are walled grave-gardens with an
    ossuary, a bell tower and a quiet hostel (Rimfolk_Plan_GraveGarden). At the gate: after a raid they take the enemy's
    dead off the colony's hands and ring for them, which the raiders' people remember (Rimfolk_Evt_BellForTheDead); a
    bell-keeper leads the funeral of one of the colony (Rimfolk_Evt_BellFuneral); with Anomaly, a bell-keeper warns that
    the ground is stirring before the void acts (Rimfolk_Evt_BellVoidSigns). Their grave-gardens ask for linen for
    shrouds in a plague year (Rimfolk_Mission_BellShrouds), and the undertakers come from them likeliest. Their founding:
    Ysolde Marr, who buried the raiders nobody would after the long war and rang a cracked bell over every grave. Faith
    (Ideology): guilt for the unburied, and the bell-keepers as a priesthood (VE Memes' exalted priesthood); the morbid
    style. Kinds of people: dirtmoles who dig, fungoids of the grave-gardens, stoneborn carvers of the ossuaries,
    forsaken and dark elves, three in four baseliners.
  -->

  <FactionDef ParentName="TribeBase">
    <defName>Rimfolk_QuietBell</defName>
    <label>Quiet Bell</label>
    <description>Keepers of the dead of every people, friend or foe. Wherever the dead lie unburied the Quiet Bell comes with spades and a small bronze bell, and every one of them is given a grave and rung for, whoever they were and whoever killed them. Between the burying they listen to the ground, for what should not stir.\n\nTheir ways began after the long war, when nobody would bury the raiders who fell in the fields, and what came up out of those fields taught the region why that matters. A widow named Ysolde Marr took a spade and a cracked chapel bell out to the fields and buried them one by one, ringing once over every grave, friend or foe, until the ground was quiet. Those who came out to help her are the first of the Bell. Every bell-keeper still carries a small bronze bell on a cord, rung once for every one of the dead.\n\nThey speak softly, little and kindly, and nothing about the dead frightens them. Their gravewardens are there for what will not stay down. Each grave-garden is kept by its sexton, and the Bell by the first bell.</description>
    <pawnSingular>bell-keeper</pawnSingular>
    <pawnsPlural>bell-keepers</pawnsPlural>
    <leaderTitle>first bell</leaderTitle>
    <techLevel>Medieval</techLevel>
    <basicMemberKind>Rimfolk_QuietBell_Keeper</basicMemberKind>
